1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,880
Two people walk into the same dealership.

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:06,010
Same car. Same price. Same day.

3
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:09,910
One of them drives home paying
fourteen thousand dollars more.

4
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:12,200
Not because he negotiated worse.

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:16,140
Because he just arrived in this
country... and the dealer knew it.

6
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:19,620
Today I'm going to show you
exactly how that happens.

7
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:22,480
With real numbers, real
government fines...

8
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:25,160
and the one rule that was
supposed to protect you,

9
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,820
until a court threw it out.

10
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:30,140
This is WorthWired.
Let's follow the money.

11
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:32,120
First, the promise.

12
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:34,940
If you're new here,
you've seen these videos.

13
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:40,100
No down payment? Doesn't matter. Just
bring your license or your passport.

14
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:42,270
From $499 a month. Brand-new vehicles.

15
00:00:42,310 --> 00:00:46,370
Miami, have you heard about our
specials? Come check them out.

16
00:00:46,410 --> 00:00:49,370
All you need is a valid
license and a bank account.

17
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:52,570
"No credit? No problem.
Drive it home today."

18
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:55,500
And here's the thing... they're not lying.

19
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:57,100
You can get approved.

20
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:00,010
The real question is: at what price?

21
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,340
When you've never had a loan in the U.S.,

22
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:06,870
the system doesn't see you as
"new." It sees you as risky.

23
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:10,300
The government estimates around
twenty-six million adults here

24
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:12,390
have no credit history at all,

25
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:15,870
and Hispanic adults are far more
likely to be in that group.

26
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:17,930
Now look at what that costs.

27
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:21,550
According to Experian, the
average used-car loan right now

28
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:23,880
is about twenty-eight thousand dollars.

29
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:27,510
Let's spread it over six
years, a very common term.

30
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,380
With good credit, the average rate
is eight point eight percent.

31
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:33,930
With no history or very low credit,

32
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:36,130
it's twenty-one point six percent.

33
00:01:36,390 --> 00:01:38,640
And don't let that
payment column fool you:

34
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:43,120
people with low credit often end up
in cheaper cars or longer loans.

35
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:47,470
Same car, same loan? The
rate is what costs you.

36
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:49,920
Same car. Same price.

37
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:54,480
With good credit, you pay about eight
thousand one hundred dollars in interest.

38
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:55,640
From $499 a month...

39
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:58,820
With no credit history,
about twenty-two thousand.

40
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:02,860
That's fourteen thousand
dollars... just for being new.

41
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:05,550
And that's before the
dealer touches anything.

42
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,270
Because inside every
dealership there's a room

43
00:02:08,310 --> 00:02:11,810
most people never think
about: the finance office.

44
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,600
And in that room, there are four traps.

45
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:17,560
Trap number one: the hidden markup.

46
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:20,140
When the dealer "finds you financing,"

47
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:23,720
the bank approves you at one
rate, called the buy rate.

48
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,510
The dealer can write your
contract at a higher rate

49
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:28,270
and keep part of the difference.

50
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,390
On our example, just two
and a half extra points

51
00:02:31,430 --> 00:02:34,840
means about twenty-five hundred
dollars more in interest.

52
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:38,410
You'll never see the bank's real
rate unless you ask for it.

53
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:41,650
And it adds up: in late
twenty twenty-five,

54
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:45,330
the average dealer was making
almost two thousand dollars per car

55
00:02:45,370 --> 00:02:46,780
in that office alone.

56
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:49,610
Not on the car. On the paperwork.

57
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,380
Trap number two: the extras.

58
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:56,680
GAP coverage. Extended
warranties. Credit insurance.

59
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:59,400
Sometimes useful, often overpriced,

60
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:03,280
and usually rolled into your loan,
so you pay interest on them too.

61
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,340
In twenty twenty-three, the
CFPB fined Toyota's lending arm

62
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:09,210
sixty million dollars.

63
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:11,700
Regulators said it pushed these extras,

64
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:15,680
which added seven hundred to twenty-five
hundred dollars to the average loan,

65
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,660
and then made them hard to cancel.

66
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:20,620
If you saw our last video,
you know the pattern:

67
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:22,980
easy to buy, hard to cancel.

68
00:03:23,130 --> 00:03:25,870
Trap number three: "Take it home today."

69
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:27,880
It's called spot delivery.

70
00:03:28,010 --> 00:03:31,150
You sign, you drive home,
you show your family.

71
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,920
Then, days later, the phone rings:

72
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:37,950
"Your financing fell through. Come
back and sign a new contract."

73
00:03:38,170 --> 00:03:39,460
At a higher rate.

74
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,720
Consumer advocates call
it yo-yo financing.

75
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:45,710
People who re-sign end
up paying, on average,

76
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:47,970
about five percentage points more.

77
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:51,490
Warning signs: blank
spaces in the contract,

78
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:54,290
a form that says the
sale is "conditional,"

79
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,640
or leaving without a
final contract at all.

80
00:03:57,790 --> 00:04:01,010
Trap number four: "We'll
pay off your old car."

81
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:03,790
If you owe more than
your trade-in is worth,

82
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:06,010
they don't make that debt disappear.

83
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:08,040
They roll it into your new loan.

84
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:10,380
This is happening more than ever.

85
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:14,910
Edmunds says nearly thirty percent of
trade-ins now carry negative equity,

86
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,630
about sixty-nine hundred
dollars on average.

87
00:04:17,770 --> 00:04:21,480
Those buyers pay a record nine
hundred forty-four dollars a month,

88
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,540
and over sixteen thousand
dollars in interest.

89
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:26,670
So who's protecting you?

90
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,400
At the end of twenty twenty-three, the
FTC created a rule to fight exactly this:

91
00:04:32,630 --> 00:04:34,460
show the full price upfront,

92
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,270
and say clearly that the
extras are optional.

93
00:04:37,650 --> 00:04:41,930
In January twenty twenty-five, a
federal appeals court threw it out.

94
00:04:42,090 --> 00:04:44,050
The rule never took effect.

95
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:48,090
Today, the only person
protecting you... is you.

96
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:50,190
And the results are showing up.

97
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:54,230
In October twenty twenty-five,
the share of subprime borrowers

98
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:57,710
at least sixty days behind on
their car loans hit a record

99
00:04:57,750 --> 00:05:00,610
in Fitch's data going back
to the early nineties.

100
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:05,800
And in twenty twenty-four, about one point
seven million cars were repossessed,

101
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,970
the most since two thousand nine.

102
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:11,440
Okay. Enough bad news.
Here's your playbook.

103
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:15,370
Five moves that put the power
back on your side of the desk.

104
00:05:15,770 --> 00:05:19,060
One: get pre-approved before you walk in.

105
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:22,040
Talk to a bank or a local
credit union first,

106
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:24,040
and ask if they work with newcomers.

107
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:27,920
Walk in with a rate in your pocket,
and the dealer has to beat it.

108
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,860
Two: ask one question:
"What's the buy rate?"

109
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:34,900
If they won't answer, that
tells you everything.

110
00:05:35,290 --> 00:05:39,720
Three: negotiate the out-the-door
price, not the monthly payment.

111
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:43,250
A low payment can hide a
longer loan and more interest.

112
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:47,820
Four: ask for every extra
in writing, line by line,

113
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:50,690
and say no to anything you didn't ask for.

114
00:05:51,090 --> 00:05:56,170
Five: never drive away with blank
spaces or a "conditional" contract.

115
00:05:56,340 --> 00:05:59,730
If the financing isn't
final, the deal isn't final.

116
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,610
Being new in this country
isn't a weakness.

117
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:05,520
Not knowing the game is. Now you know it.

118
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:07,800
Share this with someone who just arrived,

119
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,890
because it could save them
fourteen thousand dollars.

120
00:06:11,050 --> 00:06:14,410
And tell me in the comments:
what did a dealer promise you?

121
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:16,040
I'm reading every one.

122
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:19,160
This is WorthWired. See
you in the next one.
